  {
    "episode_id": 46687,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46687.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think there's a lot of concern there and a lot of it's valid. And you know, I again, You have humans involved. You have people involved at the FBI and DOJ and some of these other agencies that frankly still need to be held accountable. But we have to figure out how do we, I mean, part of what we always have to do with the federal government is figure out how we fix it, how we reform it, how we put in safeguards. So we're looking at changing the punitive measures for FBI agents when they're bringing either a five application, when they're bringing a search for it, when they're involved. Upping the criminal penalties for executives in the CIA and other intelligence agencies for when they cross that line. These are things that have never been in place before that are now being. We're also looking at changing and revising how the FISA court works, the inner workings of that. I mean, I would generally say, Greg, 85% of FISA has worked well to help protect America and our national security but there is 15% that has not worked and that's what the Durham Report gets at. What are the teeth? What are the changes? What are the safeguards to fix the abuses? That's what we're looking at.",
